<interrogationes unitas="historia">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I give a document undo and redo backed by an append-only log?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_creare historia_actum historia_revocare historia_reficere HistoriaProiectio" via="lib/scriba_documentum.c">Fill
      a HistoriaProiectio (memset it first, then memoria + mensura of
      a fixed-size state you own, plus vacare to reset it and applicare
      to apply one act, and ctx for both), and call
      historia_creare(piscina, intern, volumen, spatium, genus,
      origo_checkpoint, intervallum, proiectio) - scriba passes "" as
      spatium, "mutatio" as genus and "scriba:checkpoint" as origin. It
      returns NIHIL if a required argument or hook is missing. Each
      edit is historia_actum(h, act) with the act as a chorda (it
      returns the act's seq, or 0 if refused); undo is
      historia_revocare, redo historia_reficere. The projection memory
      always shows the state at the cursor.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I reopen a document from an existing volume?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_aperire historia_creare">Use
      historia_aperire with the same spatium, genus and projection: it
      finds the last live act, rebuilds the projection from the newest
      live checkpoint plus the acts after it, and returns to the last
      saved undo position. historia_creare is only for a NEW history:
      it does not read the volume, so on a volume that already holds
      acts it starts at cursor 0 while the old acts stay live in the
      log.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens to undone acts when I make a new edit after undo?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_actum historia_reficere historia_finis">The
      log is never truncated. When the cursor is behind the end,
      historia_actum first appends a branch marker (ramus ab=cursor),
      and every act between that seq and the marker becomes dead:
      replays skip it and historia_reficere can no longer reach it.
      historia_finis then equals the new act's seq.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I make one undo step cover several acts, like a debounced insert?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_actum_coniunctum historia_revocare historia_reficere">Append
      the first act with historia_actum and each later chunk with
      historia_actum_coniunctum: it writes a coniunctio marker before
      the act, and revocare and reficere then move over the whole joined
      group in one step. With no live act before it, a joined act is
      just a plain act.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can several documents share one volume?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_creare historia_aperire" via="lib/historia.worklog.md">Yes:
      give each a different spatium. With spatium s1 the act genus
      becomes s1/genus, the markers s1/ramus, s1/coniunctio and
      s1/cursor, and checkpoint keys s1/checkpoint/seq, and each
      history reads only its own. An empty (or NIHIL) spatium uses the
      bare names, which is what older volumes contain.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can historia_verificare detect that my code scribbled on the projection memory?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_verificare historia_sigillum_hex">Only
      if the scribble was sealed. historia_verificare replays the log
      from nothing (no checkpoint) and compares the result with the
      sigillum recorded after the last act. A divergence that a later
      act or checkpoint sealed is caught. Memory changed AFTER the last
      seal is not detected: the replay matches the recorded seal and
      silently overwrites your change.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I keep checkpoints small when my state is not one fixed block?
    <responsum symbolum="HistoriaProiectio historia_creare" via="lib/historia.worklog.md">Set
      all three hooks: sigillare (hash your raw canonical state; it runs
      after every act), codificare (the checkpoint bytes; length 0 skips
      the checkpoint) and decodificare (restore; FALSUM means unusable,
      e.g. an old format, and historia replays from nothing). Then
      memoria may be NIHIL and mensura 0. historia_creare returns NIHIL
      if only some of the three are set.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does the intervallum argument control?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_creare historia_numerus_vivorum">A
      checkpoint is written when a NEW act (historia_actum or
      historia_actum_coniunctum) brings the number of live acts to a
      multiple of intervallum (0 means LXIV); undo and redo never write
      one. Checkpoints are found by
      listing the volume's files, not by seq arithmetic, because the
      volume interleaves its own bookkeeping acts; a checkpoint inside
      dead (undone and branched-over) history is never used as a
      base.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does undo append to the log instead of only moving a cursor?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_revocare historia_reficere historia_aperire" via="lib/historia.worklog.md">So
      the undo position survives a reopen (insula-rami-plan R5): undo
      and redo append a cursor marker, and historia_aperire returns to
      the last one unless an act, a branch or a join marker came after
      it. Before this, undo then quit brought the undone text back.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep a historia open for a long editing session without its piscina growing?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_revocare historia_reficere historia_actum">No:
      everything is allocated from the piscina given to historia_creare
      or historia_aperire and never released. Every undo and every
      edit after undo re-reads the volume's acts from seq 0 into that
      piscina (undo also lists the volume's files to find a
      checkpoint), and every redo reads the acts after the cursor, so
      memory grows with log length times the number of undos. Use a
      piscina whose lifetime is the document's; to reclaim it, reopen
      with historia_aperire into a fresh piscina.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I get notified when the document changes?
    <responsum symbolum="historia_observatorem_addere HistoriaObservator historia_actum_coniunctum">Register
      a callback with historia_observatorem_addere(h, fn, ctx);
      observers run in the order added after every successful
      historia_actum, historia_actum_coniunctum, historia_revocare and
      historia_reficere. A refused
      act (empty chorda, failed append) and historia_aperire do not call
      them.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
